---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import PineTexture from "../components/PineTexture.astro";
import trio from "../assets/images/trio_watercolor_pure.jpg";

const repo = "https://github.com/OGShawnLee/pino-lang";
const tree = `${repo}/tree/main`;
const blob = `${repo}/blob/main`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Pino is a statically typed programming language that I've been building since 2024."
>
  <Hero>
    <Fragment slot="title">About Pino</Fragment>
    <p slot="lead">Pino is a statically typed programming language that I've been building since 2024.</p>
  </Hero>

  <!-- The name: text beside the one picture on this page. -->
  <section class="wrap split name" aria-labelledby="name-title">
    <div class="side">
      <p class="label">The name</p>
      <h2 id="name-title">Why "Pino"?</h2>
      <p class="text">
        Pino is named after the AutoReiv android from <em>Ergo Proxy</em>. In the show she catches the Cogito virus,
        which gives machines self-awareness and feelings, and she spends most of it wearing a pink rabbit suit. I liked
        the idea of a language with a bit of that in it: something precise that still has some play and personality.
        She's the mascot.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="main">
      <Image src={trio} alt="Re-l Mayer, Vincent Law and Pino." width={720} format="webp" quality={72} />
      <figcaption>Vincent, Re-l and Pino.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <!-- The story: heading in the margin, the text in one calm reading column. -->
  <section class="wrap split story" aria-labelledby="story-title">
    <div class="side sticky">
      <p class="label">How it started</p>
      <h2 id="story-title">The story</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="main prose">
      <p class="first">Pino didn't start as a serious project. It started with a girl, a humble laptop and a C++ course.</p>
      <p>
        In 2024, my first semester, there was a girl I liked a lot. She was struggling with C++ on a modest laptop that
        worked hard just to run its tools, and a single missing semicolon could make a whole program fail.
      </p>
      <p>
        My first plan was naive. I'd write a small, friendly language and a transpiler that turned it into C++, so she
        could write her homework the easy way and hand in the C++. It didn't take me long to see that it made no sense.
        What I wanted to give her wasn't a shortcut through one course. It was a language of her own: simple, forgiving
        about the small things, and light enough for a laptop like hers.
      </p>
      <p>
        I didn't finish it in time, and it didn't end the way I hoped. But the language kept growing, and that semester
        became its rules: no semicolons, errors that help instead of scold, and a compiler that runs well on a modest
        computer. A language can be imperfect and still be kind, and I think that's what makes it beautiful.
      </p>
      <p>
        In 2026 I ran into a Data Science student at the faculty whom I'd admired from a distance since 2024. I wanted a
        way to connect and to share what I love, and Pino came back with a new goal: to be a clean, fast alternative to
        Python for people like her.
      </p>
      <p>So Pino was built with the heart as much as with logic.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- The closing line of the story, given room to breathe. -->
  <section class="quote" aria-label="A line from the story">
    <PineTexture place="section" />
    <blockquote class="wrap">
      <p>
        “Love is in the things we build for those we cherish, even if they may never be seen. And when they are, we have
        found home.”
      </p>
    </blockquote>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap split how" aria-labelledby="how-title">
    <div class="side sticky">
      <p class="label">AI and people</p>
      <h2 id="how-title">How Pino is made</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="main prose">
      <p class="first">I want to be open about how this language is built, because AI is part of it.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>2024: by hand.</strong> The first versions of Pino were written by hand in C++.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>The move to C#.</strong> The compiler was later migrated to C#, with Gemini Flash doing most of the
          migration.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Today: AI with a human in charge.</strong> The new compiler (<a href={`${tree}/pino-csharp-re/`}
            ><code>pino-csharp-re</code></a
          >), the <a href={`${blob}/spec/SPEC.md`}>specification</a> and the conformance tests are written and heavily
          improved by Claude and Gemini. They work from small tickets, and every design decision is mine, recorded in
          <a href={`${tree}/spec/decisions/`}><code>spec/decisions/</code></a>. Nothing is merged until the full test
          suite passes. Commits written with an AI name the model in a <code>Co-Authored-By</code> line, so the history
          shows who wrote what.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The self-hosted compiler: by hand.</strong>
          <a href={`${tree}/projects/pino-compiler/`}><code>projects/pino-compiler</code></a> is written by hand, one
          piece at a time, while I read the literature, starting with Nora Sandler's <em>Writing a C Compiler</em>.
          This is where I learn how compilers really work.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The games.</strong> SABLE's concept and writing came from DeepSeek, and Antigravity helped program it,
          as noted in the <a href={`${repo}#games`}>README</a>.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>The tools have changed over the years. The reason hasn't.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* A section with its heading in a narrow left column and its content on the right. */
  .split {
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 1.5rem;
    margin-top: clamp(3.5rem, 8vw, 6rem);
    padding-top: 1.25rem;
  }
  /* The rule spans the content width, like the header's. */
  .split::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: var(--gutter);
    right: var(--gutter);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .quote + .split {
    margin-top: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 5rem);
  }
  .quote + .split::before {
    display: none;
  }
  @media (min-width: 56rem) {
    .split {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 18rem) minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 4rem;
    }
    .sticky {
      position: sticky;
      top: 2rem;
      align-self: start;
    }
    .name {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1.15fr);
      align-items: center;
    }
  }
  .side .label {
    margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
  }
  h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 3.6vw, 2.1rem);
    line-height: 1.15;
    margin-bottom: 1.4rem;
  }
  .text {
    max-width: 34rem;
    color: var(--soft);
  }

  /* Reading text. */
  .prose {
    max-width: 38rem;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
    color: var(--soft);
  }
  .prose p + p,
  .prose ul + p {
    margin-top: 1.1rem;
  }
  .prose .first {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-size: 1.15rem;
  }
  .prose ul {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 1.2rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    font-size: 0.98rem;
  }
  .prose li {
    padding: 0.35rem 0 0.35rem 1.4rem;
    text-indent: -1.4rem;
  }
  .prose li::before {
    content: "— ";
    color: var(--pine);
  }
  .prose strong {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  code {
    font-size: 0.9em;
  }

  figure {
    margin: 0;
  }
  figure :global(img) {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    border-radius: 4px;
  }
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    figure :global(img) {
      filter: brightness(0.9);
    }
  }
  figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--soft);
  }

  /* The quote: a full-width band with plenty of air around one line. */
  .quote {
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
    margin-top: clamp(4rem, 10vw, 7rem);
    padding-block: clamp(5rem, 14vw, 10rem);
    border-block: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  blockquote {
    position: relative;
    margin: 0 auto;
    text-align: center;
  }
  blockquote p {
    max-width: 44rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
    font-family: var(--font-poem);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 4.6vw, 3.1rem);
    line-height: 1.2;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    color: var(--pine);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
</style>
